 "autor": "claude",
 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "You build a house on clay, and the first dry summer splits the walls open.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Clay holds water like a sponge. When it dries, it shrinks.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And the top few meters dry out the most, right under your house.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So you patch the cracks and paint over them.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "But in winter the rain comes back, the clay swells, and the house rises.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Up in winter, down in summer. Every year the cracks get wider.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So you drill concrete piles three meters down, below the clay that moves.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Down there, the clay stays wet all year, so the piles never move.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "But the floor still cracks. The swelling clay pushes it up from below.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So you hang the floor on beams, with a gap of air underneath.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now the clay can swell all it wants without touching the house.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And one big tree can drink hundreds of liters a day from that clay.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So you plant it far from the walls.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "That's why houses in clay country sit on piles and beams.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Congratulations! You just built a house on clay. What should we build next?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  }
 ],
